ML_PAGE("l3-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l3\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 3 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Lecture 3 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 32 multiple-choice questions and 7 coding questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with answers hidden until you reveal them. Every question also has a <b>harder version</b> on the same concept, one reveal below its answer.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>32</strong><span>MCQs in the notes, each with a harder version</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>7</strong><span>Coding questions</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Bloom challenge questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l3\" data-quiz=\"3\">Start Lecture 3 simulator</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"3\" data-quiz-mode=\"hard\">Hard mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"3\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Bloom challenge</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l3\" id=\"l3p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">21 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Lecture 3 - MCQ Exam Preparation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s0\" title=\"Lecture 3 - MCQ Exam Preparation\">Overview</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - FUNDAMENTALS</li><li data-full=\"1. What you should know instantly\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s2\" title=\"1. What you should know instantly\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Key facts table</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Feature-engineering fundamentals\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s3\" title=\"2. Feature-engineering fundamentals\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Practice: fundamentals</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - NUMERIC FEATURES</li><li data-full=\"3. Numeric engineering\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s5\" title=\"3. Numeric engineering\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Binarization and rounding</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Interaction features\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s6\" title=\"4. Interaction features\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Interaction features</button></li><li data-full=\"5. Polynomial features\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s7\" title=\"5. Polynomial features\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Polynomial features</button></li><li data-full=\"6. Binning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s8\" title=\"6. Binning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Binning</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Custom binning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s9\" title=\"7. Custom binning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Custom bins</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Quantile binning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s10\" title=\"8. Quantile binning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Quantile bins</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Log transformation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s11\" title=\"9. Log transformation\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Log transform</button></li><li data-full=\"10. Box-Cox\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s12\" title=\"10. Box-Cox\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Box-Cox</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART C - CATEGORICAL FEATURES</li><li data-full=\"11. Nominal vs ordinal\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s14\" title=\"11. Nominal vs ordinal\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Nominal vs ordinal</button></li><li data-full=\"12. Label encoding trap\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s15\" title=\"12. Label encoding trap\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Label encoding trap</button></li><li data-full=\"13. One-hot encoding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s16\" title=\"13. One-hot encoding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>One-hot encoding</button></li><li data-full=\"14. Dummy coding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s17\" title=\"14. Dummy coding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Dummy coding</button></li><li data-full=\"15. Effect coding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s18\" title=\"15. Effect coding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Effect coding</button></li><li data-full=\"16. Feature hashing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s19\" title=\"16. Feature hashing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Feature hashing</button></li><li data-full=\"17. Critical train/test rule\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s20\" title=\"17. Critical train/test rule\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Fit encoders on train only</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART D - CODING &amp; REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"18. Lecture 3 coding questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s22\" title=\"18. Lecture 3 coding questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Coding questions</button></li><li data-full=\"19. Lecture 3 answer key\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s23\" title=\"19. Lecture 3 answer key\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>Answer key</button></li><li data-full=\"20. Lecture 3 traps to drill\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s24\" title=\"20. Lecture 3 traps to drill\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Top traps</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l3\" open><summary><div><strong>Lecture 3 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Know-cold tables, traps, practice questions and coding questions - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>21 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Lecture 3 - MCQ Exam Preparation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Feature Engineering: Numeric &amp; Categorical Data</h4>\n<p>I'm treating <strong>Lecture 3</strong> as the first half of the combined slides: feature-engineering fundamentals, numeric features, and categorical features. This follows the natural structure of the material: the chapter first establishes feature engineering, then covers numeric data and categorical data before moving into text.</p>\n<p>The core idea to keep in your head throughout the exam is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Raw data} \\neq \\text{good features}}$$</div>\n<p>Feature engineering tries to represent the underlying problem in a form that the algorithm can learn from effectively. The lecture explicitly argues that good features can allow simpler models to outperform complex models working with poor features.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l3p-d1\">PART A - FUNDAMENTALS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. What you should know instantly</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Immediate thought</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature engineering</td>\n<td>Turn raw information into useful model features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Iterative process</td>\n<td>Engineer → Train → Evaluate → improve features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Domain knowledge</td>\n<td>Helps decide meaningful transformations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Binarization</td>\n<td>Number/count → 0 or 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rounding</td>\n<td>Remove meaningless precision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interaction</td>\n<td>Combine features to expose joint effects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polynomial features</td>\n<td>$x_1,x_2,x_1^2,x_1x_2,x_2^2...$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Binning</td>\n<td>Continuous number → interval/category</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fixed-width binning</td>\n<td>Same numeric width</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custom binning</td>\n<td>Domain-defined boundaries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quantile binning</td>\n<td>Approximately equal observations per bin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Log transform</td>\n<td>Compress large positive values/right skew</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Box-Cox</td>\n<td>Flexible power transformation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal</td>\n<td>Categories with no order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordinal</td>\n<td>Categories with meaningful order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Label encoding</td>\n<td>Category → integer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One-hot</td>\n<td>$m$ categories → $m$ binary columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dummy coding</td>\n<td>$m$ categories → $m-1$ columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Effect coding</td>\n<td>Reference category uses -1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature hashing</td>\n<td>Categories → fixed number of hash buckets</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Feature-engineering fundamentals</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>What is the main purpose of feature engineering?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Increase the number of rows</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Transform raw data into useful representations for ML</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Always remove categorical features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Replace model evaluation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H1</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which activity is <strong>not</strong> feature engineering?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Turning a raw timestamp into hour-of-day</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Creating Attack x Defense from two existing columns</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Measuring the final model's accuracy on the held-out test set</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Log-transforming a heavily right-skewed income column</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Feature engineering <strong>transforms raw data into representations a model can use</strong>: extracting parts (A), building interactions (B) and reshaping distributions (D). Measuring accuracy on the test set (C) is <strong>evaluation</strong>. It tells you whether your features helped, but it doesn't create or change any.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>Which statement is most consistent with the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Complex algorithms always outperform simple algorithms</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Feature engineering is performed only once</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Good features can be more important than using a more complex algorithm</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Raw data should never be transformed</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H2</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Team A trains a complex model on raw, untransformed columns and gets 78% accuracy. Team B trains a simple model on carefully engineered features (log income, an interaction term, sensible bins) and gets 85%. Which lecture idea does this best illustrate?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Simple models always beat complex ones.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Good features can matter more than a more complex algorithm.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Feature engineering is a one-off step done before modelling.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Complex models need no features at all.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture's point is that <strong>representation quality</strong> often drives performance more than algorithm complexity. A over-generalises from one comparison (\"always\"). C contradicts the iterative process. D is false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>Which sequence best reflects the iterative feature-engineering process?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Engineer → Train → Evaluate → Refine</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Deploy → Delete → Encode → Train</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Evaluate → Collect → Stop</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Scale → Deploy → Ignore performance</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H3</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A team engineers new features, trains a model, and finds its validation accuracy has <strong>dropped</strong>. Following the iterative feature-engineering process, what should happen next?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Deploy anyway; the features were engineered carefully.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Delete all engineered features permanently and never try again.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Evaluate which features hurt, refine or replace them, and retrain.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Skip evaluation in future so the drop isn't seen.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The process is a loop: <strong>Engineer → Train → Evaluate → Refine</strong>, repeated. A bad result is information for the next refinement, not a reason to deploy (A), give up (B) or stop measuring (D).</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>Why is domain knowledge important?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It automatically removes all missing data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It helps identify transformations that make sense for the actual problem</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It makes test data unnecessary</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It replaces mathematics entirely</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H4</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which transformation relies <strong>most</strong> on domain knowledge rather than a generic statistical rule?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Log-transforming a right-skewed income column</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Binning blood pressure at clinical thresholds (normal / elevated / high)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Splitting a column into quartiles with <code>pd.qcut(..., q=4)</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Rounding a sensor reading to whole percentages because the readings are only accurate to about ±1%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Clinical thresholds come from <strong>medical knowledge</strong> of where risk changes, not from the shape of the data. A and C are generic, distribution-driven choices. D is a precision rule. Domain knowledge tells you which transformation fits the actual problem.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l3p-d4\">PART B - NUMERIC FEATURES</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Numeric engineering</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Binarization</h4>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>A music recommendation system only needs to know whether a user has ever listened to a song.</p>\n<p>Which transformation is most suitable?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Log transformation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Binarization</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Dummy coding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> TF-IDF</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H5</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A music service has two goals: (1) recommend songs based only on whether a user has ever played them; (2) use play counts as a feature where most users have under 20 plays but a few have more than 10,000. Which pairing of transformations fits best?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> (1) binarization, (2) log transformation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> (1) log transformation, (2) binarization</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> (1) one-hot encoding, (2) feature hashing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> (1) rounding, (2) dummy coding</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Goal (1) asks <strong>whether</strong> something happened, so binarization fits. Goal (2) keeps <strong>how much</strong> but has a long right tail, which a log transformation compresses. B swaps them. Binarizing (2) would throw away the amount information the goal needs. C and D are for categorical data.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>Given:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">listen_count = [0, 14, 0, 37, 2]\n</code></pre>\n<p>using threshold $&gt;0$, what is the binary result?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>[0, 14, 0, 37, 2]</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>[0, 1, 0, 1, 1]</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>[1, 0, 1, 0, 0]</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>[0, 0, 0, 1, 1]</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H6</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>listen_count = [0, 14, 0, 37, 2]</code> is passed through <code>Binarizer(threshold=2)</code>, which outputs 1 when a value is <strong>greater than</strong> the threshold. What is the result?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> [0, 1, 0, 1, 1]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> [1, 0, 1, 0, 0]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> [0, 14, 0, 37, 2]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> [0, 1, 0, 1, 0]</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Only values <strong>strictly greater than 2</strong> become 1: 14 → 1 and 37 → 1, while 2 → 0. A would be right for \"≥ 2\" or for the lecture's threshold of 0. B inverts the rule. C is the input unchanged. Moving the threshold changes what \"happened\" means.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<h4>Rounding</h4>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>A sensor outputs:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$87.638294\\%$$</div>\n<p>but precision beyond the nearest whole percentage is meaningless.</p>\n<p>Which transformation is most appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Binarization</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Rounding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> One-hot encoding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Hashing</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H7</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A humidity sensor is only accurate to about ±1%. It reports 87.638294%, 87.912% and 88.104%. Which treatment is most appropriate?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Truncate to 87, 87, 88, because truncation is safer than rounding.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Keep every decimal, because more precision always means more information.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Round to whole percentages, giving 88, 88, 88: the differences are smaller than the sensor's error, so they shouldn't separate the readings.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Binarize the readings at 50%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Rounding removes <strong>fake precision</strong>. With ±1% error, a gap of 0.47% between readings is noise, so all three are effectively 88%. A introduces a bias (it always rounds down) and splits readings that are indistinguishable. B is the trap the lecture warns about. D destroys the useful magnitude.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>Why might excessive decimal precision be harmful?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It may represent noise rather than meaningful signal</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Decimals cannot be processed by ML</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> All decimals indicate missing data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It forces unsupervised learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H8</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A model uses GPS coordinates stored to 10 decimal places (sub-millimetre precision, far beyond the device's real accuracy). It scores 99% on training data and 70% on test data. What is the most plausible link?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Decimals can't be processed, so the model failed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The excess decimals are mostly noise, so the model can memorise meaningless detail in the training rows. That is overfitting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The decimals turned the problem into unsupervised learning.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The model is underfitting because it has too little precision.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Precision beyond what the measurement supports carries <strong>noise, not signal</strong>, and a flexible model can latch onto that noise. That is why rounding can help generalisation. A and C are false. D contradicts the 99% training score.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Interaction features</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>Suppose model performance depends not just on Attack and Defense separately, but on having both high simultaneously.</p>\n<p>Which feature could help?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Attack}+0$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Attack} \\times \\text{Defense}$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> One-hot encode Attack</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Drop Defense</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture uses exactly the interaction:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$49\\times49=2401$$</div>\n<p>to demonstrate this idea.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H9</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Four Pokémon have (Attack, Defense) values: P (49, 49), Q (90, 10), R (60, 30), S (70, 25). Q has the highest Attack + Defense. Which has the highest Attack x Defense, and why might the product be the better feature?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Q, because the product follows the sum.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> S, because its Attack is highest among P, R and S.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> R, because the product favours balanced stats.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> P, with 2,401: the product is large only when <strong>both</strong> stats are high, capturing \"strong at both at once\".</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The products are P = 2,401, Q = 900, R = 1,800 and S = 1,750, so P is highest even though Q has the highest sum (100 vs 98). An interaction term rewards <strong>joint</strong> strength, which a sum can't express. A assumes the product ranks like the sum. B and C get the arithmetic wrong.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>Why create interaction features?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> To capture relationships between variables that may not be visible when treated independently</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> To reduce every feature to 0/1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> To remove categorical variables</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> To prevent test/train splitting</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H10</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>For which situation is an <strong>interaction feature</strong> most clearly needed?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Loan default risk is high only when debt is high <strong>and</strong> income is low at the same time.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> House price rises steadily with floor area.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A product category needs encoding.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An income column is right-skewed.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>An interaction captures an effect that exists only for a <strong>combination</strong> of variables, for example $\\text{Debt} \\times \\tfrac{1}{\\text{Income}}$ or $\\text{Debt}/\\text{Income}$. B is a single-variable trend. C calls for encoding. D calls for a log or Box-Cox transformation.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. Polynomial features</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l3_poly_count\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Feature explosion</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · How quickly polynomial features multiply.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Given:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x_1,\\ x_2$$</div>\n<p>a degree-2 expansion may create:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x_1,\\ x_2,\\ x_1^2,\\ x_1x_2,\\ x_2^2$$</div>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>What is a major danger of blindly applying polynomial features to many original columns?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Data becomes XML</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Feature explosion</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Categories become ordinal</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Missing values disappear</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture explicitly warns about feature explosion when polynomial expansion is applied to many columns.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H11</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False)</code> turns 2 features into 5 ($x_1, x_2, x_1^2, x_1x_2, x_2^2$). How many output features does it produce from <strong>3</strong> inputs, and from <strong>10</strong> inputs?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 6 and 20</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 10 and 66</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 9 and 65</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 9 and 100</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The count is the originals ($n$), plus the squares ($n$), plus the pairwise products ($n(n-1)/2$). For $n = 3$: $3 + 3 + 3 = 9$. For $n = 10$: $10 + 10 + 45 = 65$. Going from 10 inputs to 65 is the <strong>feature explosion</strong> the lecture warns about. B includes a bias column (which <code>include_bias=False</code> removes). A counts only originals and squares (and miscounts). D assumes $n^2$.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Binning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>Transforming:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Age}=37$$</div>\n<p>into:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">30-39\n</code></pre>\n<p>is called:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scaling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Binning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Stemming</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Hashing</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H12</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Ages are binned into 30-39, 40-49 and so on. After binning, which statement is true?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 39 and 40 now look more different to the model than 30 and 39, even though 39 and 40 are only one year apart.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Binning keeps every exact age, so nothing is lost.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 39 and 40 end up in the same bin.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Binning always improves accuracy.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Binning trades exact values for ranges. <strong>Within</strong> a bin, differences disappear (30 ≈ 39). <strong>Across</strong> a boundary, a tiny difference becomes a full category change (39 → 40). This is why boundaries should be chosen carefully. B and C are false. D overclaims.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>Which best describes fixed-width bins?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Each bin contains equal numbers of observations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Each bin covers equal numerical width</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Boundaries are learned only by CNNs</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Every value becomes 0 or 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H13</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Income ranges from RM1,000 to RM1,000,000 with a long right tail: most people earn under RM100,000. You create 10 fixed-width bins, each RM100,000 wide. What is the most likely result?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Each bin holds about 10% of the people.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Most people land in the first bin, while the upper bins are nearly empty.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The bins adapt to the data so that the counts balance out.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Fixed-width bins remove the skew completely.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Fixed-width bins cover <strong>equal numerical ranges</strong>, not equal counts. With skewed data, almost everyone falls in the low range. A and C describe quantile binning. D is false: the skew shows up as unequal bin counts.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>Which is fixed-width?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">0-9\n10-19\n20-29\n30-39\n</code></pre></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Lowest 25%\nNext 25%\nNext 25%\nTop 25%\n</code></pre></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture defines fixed-width binning as predefined ranges of equal or deliberately fixed numerical width.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H14</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>How do <code>pd.cut(df[\"age\"], bins=[0, 10, 20, 30, 40, 50])</code> and <code>pd.qcut(df[\"income\"], q=4)</code> differ?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both produce bins with equal numbers of rows.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>cut</code> learns its boundaries from the data; <code>qcut</code> uses the analyst's boundaries.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>cut</code> uses boundaries the analyst fixes in advance; <code>qcut</code> takes its boundaries from the data's quartiles, so each bin holds about 25% of the rows.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both always produce bins of equal width.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><code>cut</code> uses the <strong>predefined ranges</strong> you pass in (fixed or custom). <code>qcut</code> splits at <strong>quantiles</strong>, so the counts are balanced and the widths vary. B swaps them. A describes only <code>qcut</code>. D describes only fixed-width <code>cut</code>.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Custom binning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose a developer-age feature is transformed as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">0-22   Student\n22-35  Junior\n35-50  Senior\n50-100 Veteran\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>This is best described as:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Quantile binning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Custom/domain-based binning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> One-hot encoding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Standardization</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H15</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Developer ages are binned as Student 0-22, Junior 22-35, Senior 35-50 and Veteran 50-100. Which statement is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The bins are fixed-width, because each label covers a range.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> These are quantile bins, so each holds about 25% of developers.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> These are custom (domain-based) bins: the widths (22, 13, 15 and 50 years) differ on purpose to match career stages, and the group sizes aren't guaranteed to be equal.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> This is one-hot encoding.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The widths are deliberately <strong>unequal</strong>, and the boundaries come from <strong>career meaning</strong>, not from the data's quantiles. That makes them custom bins. A ignores the unequal widths. B assumes balanced counts that nobody computed. D confuses binning with encoding.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>Why can custom bins be preferable to equal-width bins?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They can reflect meaningful business or domain boundaries</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They always guarantee equal group sizes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They eliminate missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They convert data to text automatically</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H16</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>When would <strong>quantile</strong> bins be a better choice than custom bins?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> When regulations define fixed boundaries, such as tax brackets.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> When there are no meaningful domain boundaries and the data is heavily skewed, so you want groups of balanced size.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> When clinical thresholds are well established.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Never, because custom bins always win.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Custom bins are best when the <strong>domain</strong> supplies meaningful cut-points (A, C). Without such boundaries, and with skewed data, quantiles give <strong>balanced groups</strong>. D overclaims.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Quantile binning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>What does quartile binning attempt to produce?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Four bins of equal numerical width</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Four bins containing approximately 25% of the observations each</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Four random bins</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Four one-hot columns</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H17</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Values <code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 100]</code> are split into 4 bins in two ways: equal-width (<code>pd.cut(v, 4)</code>) and quartile (<code>pd.qcut(v, 4)</code>). How many values fall in each bin?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Equal-width: [2, 2, 2, 2]; quartile: [7, 0, 0, 1]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both give [2, 2, 2, 2]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both give [7, 0, 0, 1]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Equal-width: [7, 0, 0, 1]; quartile: [2, 2, 2, 2]</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Equal-width bins split the <strong>range</strong> 1-100 into four slices about 25 wide. Seven values sit in the first slice and the outlier 100 sits alone in the last. Quartile bins split the <strong>8 values</strong> into four groups of 2. A swaps the two methods. This is the lecture's fixed-vs-quantile contrast on skewed data.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>When is quantile binning especially useful?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Highly uneven/skewed distributions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Pure image data only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> When every value is identical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> When categories already have an order</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H18</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Income is heavily right-skewed. Which statement about <strong>quantile</strong> binning on this data is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> All bins have equal width.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The top bin may hold very small and very large values together.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Quantile binning only works for image data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It makes the bin counts very unequal.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Quantile bins balance the <strong>counts</strong>, so in the sparse tail one bin must stretch very wide, for example from RM9,000 to RM2,000,000. That is the price of balanced groups. A describes fixed-width bins. D is the opposite of what quantiles do. C is false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Log transformation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>Which situation most strongly suggests considering a log transformation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Country = Malaysia/Japan/UK</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Income distribution with a long right tail and a few extremely large values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Colour categories</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Missing text</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H19</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which feature is the <strong>best</strong> candidate for a log transformation?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Number of children (0-5)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Follower counts: most accounts have under 500, but a few have 50 million</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Temperature in °C, including negative values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Country of residence</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A log transformation compresses a <strong>long right tail of huge positive values</strong>, which is exactly B. A has a small range with no tail. C contains negative values, which a log can't handle directly. D is categorical.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>What is the major effect of the log transformation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Expands large values even more</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Compresses large values and reduces right skew</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Converts categories to binary</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Sorts records alphabetically</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H20</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>On a $\\log_{10}$ scale, how far apart are 1,000 and 1,000,000, compared with 10 and 100?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 999,000 vs 90, unchanged</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 3 vs 1: the huge raw gap shrinks to only three times the small one</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 6 vs 2</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1 vs 1, because logs make every gap equal</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$\\log_{10}$ maps 10 → 1, 100 → 2, 1,000 → 3 and 1,000,000 → 6. So the gaps are $6 - 3 = 3$ and $2 - 1 = 1$. A raw gap more than 11,000 times larger becomes only 3 times larger, which is how a log <strong>compresses large values</strong> and reduces right skew. C uses the endpoints instead of the gaps. D overstates the effect.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. Box-Cox</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>What is Box-Cox primarily intended to do?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Encode nominal features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Find a useful power transformation that stabilizes variance and makes the distribution more normal-like</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Detect duplicates</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Generate images</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H21</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>An analyst tries log, square-root and reciprocal transformations by hand, then keeps whichever makes the distribution most normal-like. Which method automates this idea?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Box-Cox, which searches for a power parameter $\\lambda$ whose transformation can resemble log, square-root or reciprocal.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Quantile binning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> One-hot encoding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Feature hashing</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Box-Cox is a <strong>family</strong> of power transformations, and $\\lambda$ is chosen to stabilise variance and make the data more normal-like. In other words: \"log is one specific choice; Box-Cox searches for the right one\". B, C and D don't change the shape of a continuous distribution in this way.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>What important restriction does the lecture give for Box-Cox?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$x&lt;0$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$x&gt;0$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Only integer data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Only categorical data</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture notes that Box-Cox searches for a parameter $\\lambda$ and requires strictly positive values.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H22</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which feature can Box-Cox be applied to <strong>directly</strong>, without shifting it first?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Account balance, which can be negative</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Number of purchases, which includes zeros</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> House price in RM, which is always above 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Temperature in °C</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Box-Cox requires $x &gt; 0$, <strong>strictly positive</strong>. Zero (B) fails as well as negative values (A, D). Those would have to be shifted first.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l3p-d13\">PART C - CATEGORICAL FEATURES</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. Nominal vs ordinal</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>Which is nominal?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Small, Medium, Large</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Primary, Secondary, University</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Red, Blue, Green</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Low, Medium, High</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H23</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which feature is <strong>nominal</strong> even though its values are numbers?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Postcode, such as 50450 or 10200</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Number of rooms</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Satisfaction rating from 1 to 5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Age in years</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Postcodes are <strong>labels</strong> written as digits. 50450 isn't \"more\" than 10200 in any meaningful way, so the feature is nominal. C is ordinal (ordered categories). B and D are genuine numeric quantities. Being written as a number doesn't make a feature numeric.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>Which is ordinal?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Malaysia, Japan, Brazil</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Cat, Dog, Horse</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Small, Medium, Large</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Red, Blue, Green</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Nominal categories have no meaningful ordering, while ordinal categories do.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H24</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Education level (Primary, Secondary, University) is <strong>ordinal</strong>. Which encoding is most appropriate according to the lecture?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One-hot encoding, because every category needs its own column regardless of order</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Feature hashing into 2 buckets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Random integers, such as Primary = 7, Secondary = 2, University = 5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A mapping that keeps the order, such as Primary = 1, Secondary = 2, University = 3</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Ordinal categories have a <strong>meaningful order</strong>, so an order-preserving mapping keeps that information. A throws the order away (though it isn't harmful). C imposes a wrong order. B mixes categories together for no reason.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. Label encoding trap</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l3_encoder\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Categorical encodings side by side</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Type your own categories and compare label, one-hot, dummy, effect and hashing encodings.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Action    = 0\nAdventure = 1\nSports    = 2\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>What is the main danger if these are nominal categories?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <p>Model may interpret:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Sports} &gt; \\text{Adventure} &gt; \\text{Action}$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Model cannot process integers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Values become missing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Dataset becomes too small</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture explicitly warns that numeric labels introduce false magnitude/order when used with nominal categories.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H25</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Genres are encoded as Action = 0, Adventure = 1, Sports = 2 and fed into a linear model. Which hidden assumption has the model been given?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> That Adventure lies \"between\" Action and Sports, and that the step from Action to Adventure equals the step from Adventure to Sports</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> That every genre is equally likely</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> None, because integers are neutral labels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> That genres are missing values</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Integer codes bring <strong>order and equal spacing</strong> with them. The model can learn effects such as \"Sports = 2 x Adventure\", which mean nothing for nominal categories. C is the misconception the lecture warns about. B and D are unrelated.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. One-hot encoding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>One-hot encoding of:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red\nBlue\nGreen\n</code></pre>\n<p>could represent Red as:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>0</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>3</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>[1,0,0]</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>[1,1,1]</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H26</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>pd.get_dummies</code> encodes Red, Blue and Green into columns in the order <strong>[Blue, Green, Red]</strong>. How is Green encoded, and which property must every row have?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Green = [0, 1, 0]; every row has exactly one 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Green = [0, 0, 1]; a row can contain several 1s.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Green = 2; each row holds one integer.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Green = [1, 1, 0]; rows can have two 1s.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>With the columns [Blue, Green, Red], Green sets only the <strong>middle</strong> column. In standard one-hot encoding each row belongs to exactly <strong>one</strong> category, so it has exactly one 1. B uses the wrong column order (the input order instead of [Blue, Green, Red]). C is label encoding. D is invalid.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>With $m$ categories, standard one-hot encoding creates:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1 feature</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $m-1$ features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $m$ binary features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $2m$ features</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H27</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A table has three nominal columns with 4, 10 and 50 categories, plus 5 numeric columns. After standard one-hot encoding of the nominal columns (each one replaced by its encoded columns), how many columns are there?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 66</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 8</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 64</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 69</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>One-hot encoding gives $m$ columns per variable: $4 + 10 + 50 = 64$, plus the 5 numeric columns, for <strong>69</strong>. A is the dummy-coding total ($3 + 9 + 49 + 5$). C forgets the numeric columns. B counts the original columns.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. Dummy coding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>If a variable has 5 categories, dummy coding normally creates:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5 columns</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 4 columns</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1 column</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 10 columns</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><div class=\"math-display\">$$m-1=5-1=4$$</div></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H28</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A colour variable with 5 categories is encoded with <code>pd.get_dummies(..., drop_first=True)</code>. How many columns are produced, and how is the dropped category represented?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5 columns; the dropped category is [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 4 columns; the dropped (reference) category is a row of all zeros</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 4 columns; the dropped category is [-1, -1, -1, -1]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1 column holding the values 0-4</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Dummy coding keeps $m - 1 = 4$ columns, and the reference category shows up as <strong>all zeros</strong>. C is <strong>effect</strong> coding. A and D are not dummy coding at all.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>Why is one category dropped in dummy coding?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> To provide a reference category and avoid redundant representation in some models</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because the category is invalid</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because pandas cannot store it</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> To remove missing values</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H29</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>With full one-hot encoding of three genres, $\\text{Action} + \\text{Adventure} + \\text{Sports} = 1$ in every row. Why can this be a problem for some linear and statistical models?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Any one column can be computed exactly from the other two, so the representation is redundant (the dummy variable trap).</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It creates missing values.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It makes the categories ordinal.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It produces too few columns.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>If you know two of the columns, the third is <strong>already determined</strong>. Dropping one reference column removes this redundancy, which is the purpose of dummy coding. B, C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Effect coding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>How does effect coding differ from dummy coding according to the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The reference category is represented using -1 rather than all zeros</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It produces images</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It creates $m$ columns</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It uses TF-IDF</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H30</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Genre has three categories with Action as the reference, using the columns [Adventure, Sports]. What does <strong>effect coding</strong> produce?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Action = [0, 0], Adventure = [1, 0], Sports = [0, 1]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Action = [1, 0, 0], Adventure = [0, 1, 0], Sports = [0, 0, 1]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Action = [-1, -1], Adventure = [1, 0], Sports = [0, 1]</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Action = -1, Adventure = 0, Sports = 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Effect coding is dummy coding with the reference row set to <strong>-1 instead of 0</strong>, so the other categories keep their dummy codes. A is plain dummy coding. B is full one-hot encoding. D is a single ordinal-looking column.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. Feature hashing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q31</span><div><p>A categorical feature has 100,000 unique product IDs.</p>\n<p>Which technique may reduce dimensionality to a fixed number of columns?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One-hot only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Feature hashing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Log transform</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Quantile binning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H31</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>100,000 product IDs are hashed into 1,000 buckets. What is the output width, and on average how many IDs share each bucket?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1,000 columns; about 100 IDs per bucket</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 100,000 columns; 1 ID per bucket</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1,000 columns; exactly 1 ID per bucket</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 100 columns; about 1,000 IDs per bucket</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Hashing gives a <strong>fixed</strong> number of columns, the 1,000 buckets, whatever the number of categories. With 100,000 IDs that averages $100{,}000 / 1{,}000 = 100$ per bucket, so collisions are certain. B is one-hot encoding. C ignores collisions. D swaps the numbers.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q32</span><div><p>What is the major trade-off of feature hashing?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Hash collisions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Mandatory missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It cannot handle categories</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Every category gets its own column</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H32</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Two different products land in the same hash bucket. What does the model experience, and which change reduces how often this happens?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Nothing; hashing keeps every product distinct.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> One of the products is deleted; add more rows to fix it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The model can't tell the two products apart in that column; using more buckets reduces collisions, at the cost of more columns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The two products become ordinal; one-hot encode the hash output to fix it.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A <strong>collision</strong> merges different categories into the same feature. More buckets make collisions rarer but widen the output, which is the memory-versus-precision trade-off of hashing. A denies collisions exist. B and D misdescribe what happens.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. Critical train/test rule</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"bank-question loose\"><span class=\"qno\">Q33</span><p>Which is correct?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">encoder.fit(X_train)\nX_train_enc = encoder.transform(X_train)\nX_test_enc = encoder.transform(X_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>or:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">encoder.fit(X_train)\nencoder.fit(X_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>VISTOK32Z</p></div></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l3p-d21\">PART D - CODING &amp; REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. Lecture 3 coding questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Coding Q1 - Binarization</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.preprocessing import Binarizer\n\nX = [[0], [4], [12], [0]]\n\nb = Binarizer(threshold=0)\nprint(b.fit_transform(X))\n</code></pre>\n<p>Expected result conceptually:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">0\n1\n1\n0\n</code></pre>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Anything above zero becomes active/present.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q2 - Polynomial features</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\n\npoly = PolynomialFeatures(\n    degree=2,\n    include_bias=False\n)\n\nX_new = poly.fit_transform(X)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is this doing?</p>\n<p>A. Missing-value imputation<br />\nB. Creating original, squared and interaction terms<br />\nC. One-hot encoding<br />\nD. Hashing</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: B</strong></p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q3 - fixed bins</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;age_group&quot;] = pd.cut(\n    df[&quot;age&quot;],\n    bins=[0, 20, 40, 60, 100]\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What happens?</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Continuous ages are mapped into predefined ranges.</p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q4 - quantile bins</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;income_group&quot;] = pd.qcut(\n    df[&quot;income&quot;],\n    q=4\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What does <code>q=4</code> mean?</p>\n<p>A. Width 4<br />\nB. Four quantile groups<br />\nC. Four missing values<br />\nD. Four numeric features</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: B</strong></p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q5 - log transform</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;income_log&quot;] = np.log1p(df[&quot;income&quot;])\n</code></pre>\n<p>Why might this be used?</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> To compress large positive values and reduce right skew.</p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q6 - one-hot encoding</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">pd.get_dummies(\n    df,\n    columns=[&quot;country&quot;]\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Given:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Malaysia\nJapan\nBrazil\n</code></pre>\n<p>the result broadly contains three indicator columns.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q7 - dummy coding</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">pd.get_dummies(\n    df[&quot;country&quot;],\n    drop_first=True\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>If there are four countries, how many indicator columns?</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$4-1=3$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. Lecture 3 answer key</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Q</th>\n<th class=\"ta-c\">Answer</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Q</th>\n<th class=\"ta-c\">Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">1</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">18</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">2</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">19</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">3</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">20</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">4</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">21</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">5</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">22</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">6</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">23</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">7</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">24</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">8</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">25</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">9</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">26</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">10</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">27</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">11</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">28</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">12</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">29</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">13</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">30</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">14</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">31</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">15</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">32</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">16</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">33</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">First workflow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">17</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"></td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">20. Lecture 3 traps to drill</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The ones I'd prioritize are:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Nominal vs Ordinal}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Label Encoding vs OneHot}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Fixed Width vs Quantile Binning}}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Log Transformation for strong right skew}}$$</div>\n<p>Also remember:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Fit encoders and similar transformers on <strong>training data</strong>, then reuse them.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l3-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Bloom challenge - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry, coding, hackathon, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the lecture&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"3\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Lecture 3 Bloom challenge</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"3\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>A nominal feature has <strong>m = 5</strong> categories. How many columns do one-hot, dummy and effect coding produce?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5, 4, 4</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 5, 5, 4</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 4, 4, 5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1, 4, 5</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>One-hot uses <strong>m</strong> columns. Dummy coding drops a reference level, giving <strong>m - 1</strong>. Effect coding also uses <strong>m - 1</strong>, but codes the reference as -1s instead of 0s. D starts with label encoding's single column, which isn't one-hot.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>What restriction does the lecture give for the Box-Cox transformation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The feature must be categorical.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The values must be integers.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The feature must already be normally distributed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The values must be strictly positive (x &gt; 0).</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Box-Cox involves $x^\\lambda$ and $\\log x$, which need <strong>x &gt; 0</strong>. A and B are false. C has it backwards - Box-Cox is used to make data <em>more</em> normal-like.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which pandas function creates bins that each hold <strong>roughly the same number of observations</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>pd.qcut</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>pd.cut</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>pd.get_dummies</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>np.log1p</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><code>qcut</code> makes <strong>quantile</strong> bins (equal counts). <code>cut</code> makes bins from given or equal-width edges. <code>get_dummies</code> one-hot encodes, and <code>log1p</code> is a transform.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>For \"has this user shown any interest in the song?\", why can binarising <code>listen_count</code> beat keeping the raw count?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Binary features always train faster, which improves accuracy.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Raw counts cannot be stored as integers.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The question is about <strong>presence</strong>: 0 → 1 matters a lot, while 37 → 38 is mostly noise, so the raw magnitude adds variation the target doesn't depend on.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Binarisation removes all users who never listened.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Feature engineering means representing what the <strong>problem</strong> needs. If the target depends on whether a user listened at all, the magnitude is irrelevant and only adds noise. A confuses speed with accuracy. B is false. D is wrong - zero-listen users stay as 0.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why does the lecture call feature engineering <strong>iterative</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because every feature must be recomputed once per epoch.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because model errors reveal what the representation is missing (e.g. weekend mistakes suggest an <code>is_weekend</code> feature), so you engineer, train, evaluate and engineer again.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because polynomial features must be added one degree at a time.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because CRISP-DM forbids changing features after modelling.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The loop is <strong>engineer → train → evaluate → new feature ideas</strong>. Evaluation tells you where the representation fails. A and C are mechanical misreadings, and D is the opposite of CRISP-DM's iterative arrows.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>For a linear model on incomes ranging from RM25,000 to RM1,000,000, why can a log transform help?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It compresses large values far more than small ones, so a few extreme incomes stop dominating the fit, while the order is preserved.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It deletes outliers from the dataset.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It guarantees a perfectly normal distribution.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It lets the model use zero and negative incomes.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$\\log_{10}$ maps 10 → 1, 1,000 → 3 and 1,000,000 → 6, which pulls the long right tail inward. Rows aren't deleted (B). Normality isn't guaranteed (C). And log is <strong>undefined</strong> at zero and below (D) - that is why <code>log1p</code> or Yeo-Johnson exist.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p><code>PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False)</code> is applied to <strong>3</strong> features. How many output columns?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 6</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 12</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 10</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 9</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>3 originals + 3 squares + 3 pairwise products = <strong>9</strong>. In general this is $\\binom{n+d}{d} - 1 = \\binom{5}{2} - 1 = 9$. C counts the bias column. A forgets the cross terms. This growth is the lecture's \"feature explosion\".</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p><code>pd.get_dummies(df[\"genre\"], drop_first=True)</code> is applied to the categories Action, Adventure and Sports. What does a row with genre <strong>Action</strong> become?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Adventure = 0, Sports = 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Action = 1, Adventure = 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Adventure = -1, Sports = -1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Adventure = 1, Sports = 0</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><code>drop_first</code> removes the first category in sorted order, <strong>Action</strong>, which becomes the reference represented by all zeros. C is effect coding. B keeps the dropped column. D is how Adventure is encoded.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>Box-Cox selects <strong>λ = 0</strong> for an income feature. Which transform is applied?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Reciprocal, 1/x</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Natural log, ln(x)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Square root</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No change</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Box-Cox is $(x^\\lambda - 1)/\\lambda$ for $\\lambda \\ne 0$ and $\\ln x$ at $\\lambda = 0$. That is why the lecture says Box-Cox can <strong>act like a log transform</strong>. λ = 0.5 gives a square-root-like transform, λ = -1 a reciprocal-like one, and λ = 1 only shifts the values by 1.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A right-skewed income feature is binned two ways:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Counts in the 4 bins</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fixed-width</td>\n<td>235 · 48 · 14 · 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quantile</td>\n<td>75 · 75 · 75 · 75</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Which reading is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fixed-width is better, because the counts reveal the skew.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both are equivalent, because both have four bins.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Fixed-width spends most bins on the sparse tail; quantile bins give each bin enough data, at the cost of uneven, data-dependent boundaries.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Quantile binning lost information, because the counts are identical.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>That is the lecture's exact trade-off. Equal widths leave tail bins nearly empty on skewed data. Quantile bins <strong>adapt</strong> to the distribution but their edges are no longer round, meaningful numbers. A confuses describing the data with representing it usefully. D misreads equal counts as lost information.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Training data has colours {Red, Blue, Green}; the test batch has {Red, Purple}. You call <code>pd.get_dummies</code> <strong>separately</strong> on each. What goes wrong?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Nothing - get_dummies always returns the same columns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Purple is automatically mapped to Red.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The training data gets an extra Purple column.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The column sets differ (the test set has <code>Purple</code> and lacks <code>Blue</code> and <code>Green</code>), so the model receives a mismatched feature matrix.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Each call learns categories from <strong>its own</strong> data. That is the lecture's critical train-vs-unseen rule. Fit an encoder on training data and reuse it, for example <code>OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\"ignore\")</code>, which maps unseen Purple to all zeros. A, B and C are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A linear model predicts battle wins from Attack and Defense. It underpredicts when <strong>both</strong> are high and overpredicts when only one is high. What does this pattern suggest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The two features interact; add Attack x Defense.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The features need min-max scaling.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The model is overfitting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Attack should be binarised.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>An additive model gives each feature a fixed contribution. Errors that depend on the <strong>combination</strong> are the signature of an interaction, which is the lecture's Pokémon example. Scaling (B) doesn't change what an additive model can represent. C doesn't match a systematic pattern. D throws information away.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Education has the levels Primary &lt; Secondary &lt; Diploma &lt; Degree. For a linear model, which encoding is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One-hot, because label-style encoding is always wrong.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Ordinal mapping (1-4) in the domain order, because the order is real and one column carries it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Alphabetical label encoding, because it is automatic.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Feature hashing.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture's rule is to ask <strong>nominal or ordinal?</strong> first. For genuinely ordered levels, a domain-defined mapping keeps the order. A over-applies the nominal rule; one-hot would also work but discards the order the model could use. C would order Degree &lt; Diploma &lt; Primary &lt; Secondary, which is meaningless. D is for high cardinality.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A logistic regression must use <code>product_id</code> with 250,000 distinct values on a memory-constrained server. Which choice is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One-hot encoding, because it is exact.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Label encoding, because it uses one column.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Feature hashing into a fixed number of buckets, accepting some collisions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Drop the feature, because high-cardinality columns are useless.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The lecture introduces hashing for <strong>thousands of categories</strong>: a fixed output size at the cost of collisions. A creates 250k columns. B imposes a false order on IDs, which a linear model treats as magnitude. D may discard strong signal.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Insurance regulations define the age bands 18-25, 26-40, 41-65 and 66+, and premiums must be explained by band. Which binning fits best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Custom bins matching the regulatory bands.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Quantile bins, because they balance the counts.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Fixed-width 10-year bins.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No binning, because exact age is always better.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture's <strong>custom binning</strong> principle: domain knowledge beats arbitrary mathematics when boundaries carry meaning. Quantile edges (B) would shift with the data and not match the regulation. C ignores the mandated bands. D conflicts with the explanation requirement.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>A dataset has <code>listen_count</code> (only presence matters), <code>income</code> (strongly right-skewed), <code>city</code> (30 unordered values) and <code>size</code> (S &lt; M &lt; L). Which feature design is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Log <code>listen_count</code>; binarise income; ordinal-map city; one-hot size.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Leave everything raw and let the model sort it out.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Binarise <code>listen_count</code>; log-transform <code>income</code>; one-hot <code>city</code>; ordinal-map <code>size</code>.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> One-hot every column, including income.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Each technique matches each column's nature - presence → <strong>binarise</strong>; skew → <strong>log</strong>; nominal → <strong>one-hot</strong>; ordered → <strong>ordinal mapping</strong>. A mismatches every pair. B ignores the point of the lecture. D one-hots a continuous variable, creating thousands of columns.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>You want to know whether a new interaction feature truly helps. Which experiment is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Add it and check whether <strong>training</strong> accuracy improves.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Add it, retrain, and compare performance on held-out validation data against the same model without it; keep it only if it generalises better.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Add it only if it has the largest coefficient.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Add every possible interaction at once and keep the model with the highest training score.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>This is the iterative loop, judged on <strong>unseen data</strong>. A and D reward fitting more, which a new feature almost always does on training data - the overfitting trap. C confuses coefficient size, which depends on scale, with predictive value.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Design categorical encoding that won't break when production sends a category never seen in training.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Call <code>pd.get_dummies</code> on each production batch.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Fit <code>OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\"ignore\")</code> on training data, save it with the model, and only <code>transform</code> test and production data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Refit the encoder on each production batch so it always knows every category.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Label-encode in production, because unseen categories get the next integer.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Fit once on training data, reuse everywhere - the lecture's critical rule, plus explicit handling of unseen values. A and C re-learn the categories, so the columns differ from training. D gives an unseen category a number the model never learned, with a fake order.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>In \"entity embeddings\", a neural network learns a dense vector for each category (for example each of 5,000 stores) during training. What advantage do they have over one-hot encoding?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Similar categories can learn nearby vectors, sharing statistical strength in a low-dimensional space, instead of 5,000 independent, equally distant columns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They need no training data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They guarantee an ordinal order.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They remove the need for a train/test split.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>One-hot makes every category <strong>equally different</strong> from every other - the same limitation Lecture 4 shows for words. Learned embeddings place behaviourally similar stores close together, which helps rare categories. It is the categorical version of word embeddings. B, C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>100 distinct categories are hashed uniformly into 1,000 buckets. Approximately what is the probability that <strong>at least one</strong> pair collides?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> About 0.10</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> About 0.50</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> About 0.99</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>This is the birthday problem: $P(\\text{no collision}) = \\prod_{i=0}^{99}(1 - i/1000) \\approx e^{-100 \\cdot 99 / 2000} \\approx 0.006$, so $P(\\ge 1) \\approx 0.994$. On average about $\\binom{100}{2}/1000 \\approx 5$ pairs collide. Collisions are near-certain long before buckets run out, which is the lecture's trade-off made concrete.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p>How many columns does this produce?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\nPolynomialFeatures(degree=3, include_bias=False).fit_transform(X)  # X has 10 columns\n</code></pre></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 30</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 285</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 165</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The number of monomials of degree 1-3 in 10 variables is $\\binom{10+3}{3} - 1 = 286 - 1 = 285$. A counts only powers of single variables. B is $10^3$, which counts ordered products. D is wrong because degree-1 and degree-2 terms are also included. Going from 10 to 285 features is the lecture's <strong>feature explosion</strong>.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Hackathon</span></div><p>In a hackathon, you replace <code>merchant_id</code> with the <strong>mean fraud rate of that merchant over the whole training set</strong>. CV shows 0.98, the leaderboard 0.71. Why?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Merchant IDs have too few categories.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Target encoding must use the median.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Each row's encoding included its own label, which leaked the target; for rare merchants it nearly equals the label. Use out-of-fold target encoding with smoothing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The leaderboard uses a different metric.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Target encoding computed on the same rows it encodes <strong>contains the answer</strong>. Out-of-fold encoding computes each row's value from other folds only, and smoothing $\\frac{n\\bar y + m\\,p}{n+m}$ pulls rare categories toward the prior $p$. It's the lecture's \"fit only on what you're allowed to see\" rule applied <em>within</em> the training data.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Quantile income bins were fitted on last year's training data. This year, nominal incomes rise about 30%, and most customers now fall into the top bin. What is happening?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Quantile bins re-adapt automatically at prediction time.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Fitted bin edges are fixed parameters. Under distribution shift they stop splitting the population evenly, so drift must be monitored and the bins (and model) refitted.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The bins are fine; only the model is wrong.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Fixed-width bins would be immune.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Quantile edges are <strong>learned from training data</strong> and then reused, which is the consistency rule. When the distribution shifts, reused edges no longer represent quartiles. A is the trap - transform doesn't refit. D is false; any fixed edges suffer under shift.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>A true risk relationship is smooth in age. An analyst bins age into 5 bins before fitting. What is the main statistical cost?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> None - binning always reduces variance with no downside.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Within-bin variation is discarded, and the model becomes a step function with artificial jumps at the bin edges, losing information and power.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It increases the number of features exponentially.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It makes the feature nominal, so ordering is lost.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture says binning trades precision for abstraction. The extension is the <em>price</em>: a step function forces people at 39.9 and 40.1 into different risk levels, and ignores the gradient inside each bin. Binning helps when the boundaries are meaningful (D15) or the relationship really is stepwise. A is false. C confuses binning with polynomial expansion. D is false - bins can stay ordinal.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>The true target is $\\text{Attack} \\times \\text{Defense}$. Which model <strong>cannot</strong> capture it without an explicit interaction feature?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A deep decision tree.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A linear regression on Attack and Defense only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A neural network with hidden layers.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A gradient-boosted ensemble of depth-6 trees.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A linear model is <strong>additive</strong> in its inputs, so it can't represent a product unless you engineer the column - which is exactly why the lecture creates Attack x Defense. Trees with depth ≥ 2 and neural networks can learn interactions themselves (A, C, D). An ensemble of depth-1 stumps, by contrast, would also be additive.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p><code>monthly_profit</code> is right-skewed but contains zeros and negative values. Which transformation is most appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Box-Cox, because it chooses λ automatically.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Yeo-Johnson, a power-transform family defined for all real values, with λ chosen by maximum likelihood.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Plain log transform.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Binarisation, because skewed data must be binned.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Box-Cox requires <strong>x &gt; 0</strong>, which is the lecture's stated restriction, so A fails. Log fails at zero and below (C). Yeo-Johnson extends the Box-Cox idea to zero and negative values. D throws away magnitude unnecessarily.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Label-encoding a nominal feature is \"dangerous\" in the lecture. How dangerous is it for a <strong>gradient-boosted tree</strong> compared with a <strong>linear</strong> model?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Equally harmful for both, because both read the codes as magnitudes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Harmless for both.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Much less harmful for trees: they split on thresholds and can isolate categories with several splits, though arbitrary codes can need more splits. A linear model treats the codes as true magnitudes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> More harmful for trees than for linear models.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The danger is the <strong>implied magnitude and order</strong>. Linear models multiply the code by a weight, so the danger is direct. Trees only use order to place thresholds, so arbitrary codes cost extra splits but don't impose a linear effect. That's why many boosting libraries accept categorical codes directly. The lecture's rule is still the safe default for linear and distance-based models.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>A ridge regression (L2-regularised, with an intercept) uses <strong>full</strong> one-hot encoding of a 4-level feature, without dropping a column. Which statement is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It fails because the matrix is singular.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is acceptable: regularisation makes the system solvable despite the collinearity, and all levels are treated symmetrically. Ordinary least squares without regularisation is where dummy coding matters most.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Effect coding is required for any regularised model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Dummy coding must always be used, regardless of the model.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The dummy-variable trap is <strong>perfect collinearity</strong> between the one-hot columns and the intercept. Unregularised OLS then has no unique solution, which is why the lecture motivates dummy coding. An L2 penalty adds $\\lambda I$, making the system invertible, so full one-hot is common with regularised models. A ignores the penalty. C and D are overgeneralisations.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A regulator requires that, all else equal, a <strong>higher income can never lower</strong> the predicted credit approval. Which design meets this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Log-transform income; that guarantees monotonicity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Quantile-bin income into 10 bins.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Drop income from the model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Train a gradient-boosted model with a monotonic (increasing) constraint on income, and verify it with partial-dependence checks.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Monotonic constraints restrict every tree split so the effect of income can only increase, which enforces domain knowledge directly. A keeps the order of the feature, but the <em>model</em> can still learn a non-monotonic response. B can produce non-monotonic bin effects. C loses predictive power. It's feature engineering's \"domain knowledge\" principle turned into a model constraint.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Hackathon</span></div><p><code>device_model</code> has 3,000 values; 2,600 of them appear fewer than 10 times, and the test set will contain unseen models. Which plan is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One-hot all 3,000 values.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Drop the rows with rare devices.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> On the training data, map categories with fewer than 10 occurrences to <code>\"Other\"</code>; fit the encoder on that; at prediction time, map unseen values to <code>\"Other\"</code> as well.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Label-encode in order of first appearance.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Grouping rare levels shrinks the number of columns, reduces overfitting on tiny categories, and gives unseen values a safe home. Learning the threshold from <strong>train only</strong> keeps it consistent. A explodes in width and overfits. B deletes data and fails on unseen values anyway. D imposes a false order.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
